<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vijayawada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bezawada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vijayawada is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the head of the Krishna delta. Known historically as Bezawada, it was a centre of the Vishnukundina dynasty, and it has the hill temple of Kanaka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> and rock-cut cave temples at Undavalli and Mogalrajapuram nearby. The Prakasam Barrage there irrigates much of the delta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vijayawada.jpg" alt="Vijayawada" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luke John · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prakasham_Barriage,_Vijayawada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vijayawada lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> at an altitude of 11 m (36 ft) above sea level, flanked by the Eastern Ghats and the Indrakeeladri Hills. Three canals that leave the north side of the reservoir behind the Prakasam Barrage, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eluru">Eluru</a>, Bandar and Ryves canals, flow through the city. The climate is tropical wet and dry, with the hottest weather in May and the coolest in January, and rain from both the south-west and north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoons</a>.</p><p>The name is explained by several legends. In one, the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> killed the demon Mahishasura and rested here, so that the place became the land of victory, from vijaya, victory, and wada, place. In another, the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> prayed on the Indrakeeladri hill and received the weapon Pashupatastra from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>; one of Arjuna's names is Vijaya. The place was called Vijayavatika before it became Vijayawada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bezawada, as the city was then known, is said to have been founded around 626 CE by the Paricchedi kings, and it was ruled by Madhava Varma, one of the kings of the Vishnukundina dynasty. The Chinese Buddhist scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> stayed there for a few years around 640 CE to copy and study the Abhidhamma Pitaka, the last of the three baskets of the Pali canon, the scriptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>.</p><p>The Mogalrajapuram hills hold five rock-cut temples built between the fourth and ninth centuries, and some of the caves can be attributed to the Vishnukundina dynasty. The Akkana Madanna Caves at the foot of Indrakeeladri Hill are a monument of national importance. In the modern period the Vijayawada Municipal Corporation was constituted on 1 April 1888, the same year the Vijayawada Junction railway station was established, and a line to Hyderabad was laid in 1889. Andhra Cements, founded in 1937, was the first cement factory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vijayawada is the second most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, with 1,021,806 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, most of them Telugu speakers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, with Muslim, Christian and Jain minorities. It is a trading centre, notable for the mango market at Nunna, and an important rail and road junction. Its Hindu festivals include Durga Pooja and a boat festival, the Theppotsavam, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> during Dussera, centred on the Kanaka Durga Temple on Indrakeeladri hill. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondapalli">Kondapalli</a> toys, handmade wooden toys from a suburb of the city, received a geographical indication in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vijayawada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut cave temples at Undavalli and Mogalrajapuram near <b>Vijayawada</b> are attributed to their period.</blockquote><a href="/state/vishnukundina-dynasty">Vishnukundina dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
